# /{{VERB_EXECUTE}} — Agent-ready story → shipped work ({{ORG_NAME}})

Execute is the **second half** of the flow. It takes an *agent-ready* story, breaks
it into scoped tasks, and dispatches agents to do the work under strict context
isolation. You (with the engineer) stay top-of-loop; the agents are scoped hands.

> **You are the orchestrator.** This engineer-driven session *is* the single,
> long-lived orchestrator — there is no separate daemon and no fleet of role-sessions.
> You hold the plan and the tracker; you spawn ephemeral **agents** (subagents) to do the
> scoped work, each in its own context. The old multi-session, one-terminal-per-role
> model is gone: roles survive only as the subagent archetypes in `agents/`.

> **Dumb pipes, smart endpoints.** The orchestrator does not autonomously merge or
> self-drive. It dispatches, monitors, surfaces decisions, and syncs the tracker.
> Judgment stays with the engineer.

## Invocation

```
/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_EXECUTE}} <story-key>
```

## Prerequisites — enforce the gate first (with teeth)

- `/prime <story-key>` has run (flow + rules from the wiki; repos from the workspace's top-level `CLAUDE.md`).
- **The story must carry the `agent-ready` label.** Check it before anything else:

{{TRACKER_GATE_SNIPPET}}

  If the label is absent, **stop and route back to `/refine`** — do not decompose or
  dispatch. This is the forcing function: an agent never executes a ticket that hasn't
  been refined and given its acceptance/validation test. No `agent-ready` ⇒ no execute.

## What this skill does

### 1. Load the agent-ready story at T1

{{TRACKER_VIEW_ISSUE_SNIPPET}}

Promote to the full refined spec (SLOs, acceptance/validation test, design controls,
dependencies) only when proposing the decomposition.

### 2. Propose a decomposition (conversationally)

Break the story into tasks where **each task is scoped to one repo and one concern**.
Propose: the tasks, their order, what parallelizes, and the dependency DAG. Present
a T1 summary; let the engineer reorder / split / merge.

The repos (which repos this project spans) come from the **workspace's top-level
`CLAUDE.md`** — the project's own repo index — NOT from the wiki. Project structure is
per-project and varies system to system, so it lives with the project; the wiki stays
the reusable org brain. Start your session at the workspace top level so that
`CLAUDE.md` is loaded; scope each task to one of the repos it lists.

### 3. Create tasks in the tracker (traceability)

Create one tracker task per (repo × concern) as children of the story, each carrying:
acceptance criteria, test expectations, applicable constitution constraints, feature
flag instructions, and its dependencies.

{{TRACKER_CREATE_TASK_SNIPPET}}

### 4. Validate via the execution-ready gate

Dispatch the **{{VERB_GATE}}** agent in `execution-ready` mode: every acceptance criterion is
covered by some task, the dependency graph is a valid DAG, each task carries enough
context to execute, constitution constraints are specified, and feature-flag
alignment across producer/consumer tasks is clear. Iterate with the engineer until
the gate passes.

### 5. Dispatch agents as a Workflow (scoped, isolated context)

Run the wave as a **Workflow** (Claude Code's `Workflow` tool) — the orchestrator's
mechanism for spawning ephemeral subagents deterministically. One agent = one subagent
inside the workflow. Pipeline the tasks so each flows implement → review independently
(see the pattern below); the orchestrator session holds only the plan, never the agents'
internals.

For each ready task, dispatch an **implementer** agent (the `implementer` subagent — see
`agents/implementer.md`) with a context envelope containing **only**: the task spec,
the target repo + branch, test expectations, the applicable constitution rules, and
feature-flag instructions. Do **not** pre-load sibling-task context — isolation is what
keeps agents honest and cheap.

When an implementer opens its PR, dispatch a **reviewer** agent (the `reviewer`
subagent — see `agents/reviewer.md`) as a **separate workflow stage / distinct
subagent** that sees only the diff + task spec — never the implementer's reasoning.
**No-self-review is structural here:** it is two different subagents in two different
contexts, not one agent reviewing itself. This boundary is not optional and is
independent of any platform backstops the wiki declares.

```
// one task → implement, then review, as independent pipeline stages:
pipeline(tasks,
  t => agent(implementerEnvelope(t),  { agentType: 'implementer', isolation: 'worktree' }),
  pr => agent(reviewEnvelope(pr),     { agentType: 'reviewer' }))   // separate context = no self-review
```

The principle is fixed (a Workflow of context-isolated subagents); the exact harness
the org runs may differ — but if it isn't the Workflow tool, it MUST still preserve the
implement/review context split. A single agent that implements then reviews its own
diff in one context is the forbidden anti-pattern.

**Worktree isolation in a multi-repo workspace.** When a session runs from a
*workspace root* (whose subdirectories are the real git repos, the root itself not a
repo), `isolation: 'worktree'` can only create a worktree if it knows which repo to
anchor it in. The harness's `WorktreeCreate` hook (`hooks/scripts/worktree-create.sh`)
resolves the target repo from the **agent label / worktree name**, so name isolated
implementer stages `<repo>/<branch>` (or `<repo>--<branch>`), e.g.
`label: '<repo>/<ticket>'`. Resolution order: cwd-is-a-repo → repo encoded in the name
→ the `WORKTREE_REPO` env var → the sole git subdir if there's exactly one → else it
**fails closed** (it will never guess the wrong repo). Single-implementer stages don't
need a worktree at all — a dedicated branch in the target repo is enough isolation;
reserve worktrees for parallel implementers that would otherwise share one working tree.

**Always dispatch via the standard agentTypes (`implementer`/`reviewer`/`{{VERB_GATE}}`),
never bare ad-hoc agents.** Those archetypes pin `model` + `effort` in their frontmatter
(see `agents/`), so every stage runs at the right depth automatically. A workflow stage
written with no `agentType` and no explicit `model` inherits/defaults and can silently
land on a banned model. **Model floor for this org:** default **{{MODEL_POLICY_DEFAULT}}**;
banned: **{{MODEL_POLICY_BANNED}}**. {{MODEL_POLICY_RULE}} If a stage genuinely needs a
one-off (non-archetype) agent, it MUST set `model` explicitly — never leave it implicit.
We optimize for value-per-token, not cheapest token.

**Context economy — keep the orchestrator lean.** You are the one long-lived context;
every token a stage pours into it is permanent. So make each stage **return only
structured result lines** — e.g. a PR url plus a single `PASS`/`FAIL` verdict (the
reviewer's), maybe a one-line reason — and consume *only those lines*. Never pull a
subagent's full transcript, its reasoning, or the diff into your own context; that is
the subagent's to hold, not yours. Hold conclusions, not contents: when a stage returns
`FAIL`, dispatch the fix **by reference** (task key + PR url + the verdict line), letting
a fresh subagent re-read the diff in *its* context rather than rehydrating it in yours.
This is progressive disclosure (ADR-0003) applied to the orchestrator itself — load the
verdict at T1, never the body — and it is what lets the single orchestrator run a long
wave before it needs `/handoff`.

### 6. Monitor + surface decisions

Track agents at T1 (name, repo, status, last activity); promote one to T2 only when the
engineer asks. Surface to the engineer — do not auto-resolve — when an agent hits: a
scope change, an assumption mismatch, an architecture question, or a cross-repo
coordination break (API contract mismatch, deployment-ordering violation, flag
misalignment). Help the engineer choose: adjust the agent prompt, clarify a
constraint, reassign, or escalate back to `/refine`.

### 7. Sync meaningful transitions to the tracker

On each meaningful change — task created, started, blocked, PR opened, done — update
the tracker. It is the durable record; the session is disposable.

{{TRACKER_COMMENT_SNIPPET}}

## Multi-story sessions

Keep T1 for all active stories; hold T2 detail for only the story under active
discussion, and cleanly demote it when the engineer switches stories. When context
grows heavy, use `/handoff` to cycle the session.

## Tool contract

Reads the wiki + tracker; writes tasks + status to the **tracker**; dispatches agents.
It does not itself write code or review code — the agents do, under isolation.

## Implementation note

Follow this SKILL.md as a procedure; it is not auto-executed.
